- Packed sizes for the 0.99 counts are estimates from the measured ratio of about 1.0 to 1.2 bytes per atom (positions delta-coded, kernel id, 8-bit amplitude, lzma) on the saved SplatPursuit atom sets at the same score levels; exact bytes at 0.95 to 0.98 are in `data/atom_bytes.json`. (12 October 2026)
- Against the region passes: 1.4 million points at 20 B is about 28 MB, roughly 17 times smaller than one 485 MB pass and about 45 percent fewer elements than 2.5 million polygons; for Bump it is about 130 times smaller. (12 October 2026)
- The oracle bound agrees (`data/residual_stats.json`, 12 October 2026): keeping the exact top-N residual pixels needs 1 456 949 pixels for Cavity at 0.99, 1 695 079 for Specular, 1 473 844 for NormalR, but only 111 405 for Bump. JPEG 8x8 block structure is not the cause (block-edge energy ratio 0.99 to 1.01), nor is 8-bit quantisation (ceiling 0.9977 on Specular, above 0.9998 elsewhere). The residual is real per-pixel grain of the scan, and Pearson at 2048 px demands that exact noise realisation; a procedural noise with matched statistics would lower Pearson, not raise it. (12 October 2026)
- Specular cannot borrow Cavity's elements: even the reference Cavity, affinely mapped, only gives r = 0.988 on Specular, below 0.99. (12 October 2026)

## Copernicus implementation, checked in the reference (12 October 2026)

- Recommended: `cop/stamppoint` (Stamp Points). Points come in from SOPs (or `cop/sopimport`); parameters Blend Method = Add (`blend`, menu entry Add; there is also Subtract), Wrap Mode = On (`wrap`) with Tile Size matching the image, Space = Image Space, stamp layer = a small Gaussian built procedurally in the graph (no image import), per-point scale from pscale and per-point value from Cd as amplitude. Signed amplitudes: two stamp nodes (Add for positive, Subtract for negative) or one with a bias removed afterwards. Segments: same node with stretch and angle attributes (`cop/configurestamps` provides Stretch X/Y and Angle), so a segment is still one point. Then a Blend (add) onto the current height. (12 October 2026)
- `cop/rasterizecurves` (Units = Pixels, Width Attribute `width`, rasterises an arbitrary attribute such as a value) and `cop/layerfromcurves` (Width, Profile ramp) can draw cracks as curves, but overlaps resolve by depth sample, not by summation, so an additive residual model would need non-overlapping curve sets; fine for a few long cracks, not for this grain. (12 October 2026)
- `cop/rasterizegsplats` reads P, Cd, GS_Alpha, orient, scale, but composites by alpha (front to back), not additively, so it does not reproduce a signed additive sum without workarounds; Stamp Points with Add is the exact match of what was simulated. (12 October 2026)
- Node count per layer: SOP points (or sopimport) + 1 or 2 stamppoint + 1 small Gaussian kernel generator + 1 blend. Not verified in Houdini (Houdini busy, per instructions). (12 October 2026)

## Recommendation (12 October 2026)

- Bump: adopt splat pursuit as a final layer (about 180 000 to 200 000 points, a few MB, 0.947 to 0.99), after improving the reproduced displacement it derives from. (12 October 2026)
- NormalR: let the running pipeline finish first; the splat layer should then only carry the pixel residual, likely well under 1 million points. (12 October 2026)
- Cavity and Specular: 0.99 at 2048 px is reachable only by encoding about a third of the pixels individually, which is honest to call a stored image in vector form. If that is acceptable, the cheapest form is points with 8-bit amplitude (about 1.5 to 1.9 MB packed, 28 to 32 MB raw bgeo). Otherwise the target metric for these two maps should be stated at a scale above the grain (for example Pearson after a 2x box downsample, or band-limited Pearson), which is Matthieu's call, not mine. (12 October 2026)
